WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:05.200
A drug clears amyloid from the brain.

2
00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:07.960
Does that help the person whose brain it is?

3
00:00:08.883 --> 00:00:12.263
The antibody lecanemab clears amyloid plaques.

4
00:00:13.063 --> 00:00:17.003
In a large trial, people with early Alzheimer disease got it or a placebo.

5
00:00:18.133 --> 00:00:20.353
Both groups were followed for a year and a half.

6
00:00:21.267 --> 00:00:25.007
The outcome was a clinician's rating of thinking and daily function.

7
00:00:25.927 --> 00:00:28.667
It runs from zero to eighteen, and higher means worse.

8
00:00:29.627 --> 00:00:32.107
The graph shows each group's change from its starting score.

9
00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:34.220
Here is a question.

10
00:00:35.140 --> 00:00:36.420
Did the treated group get better?

11
00:00:42.433 --> 00:00:44.973
No. Both lines rise.

12
00:00:46.013 --> 00:00:47.013
Both groups got worse.

13
00:00:47.813 --> 00:00:49.593
The treated group got worse more slowly.

14
00:00:50.633 --> 00:00:54.553
To compare them, take the gap as a share of the placebo group's decline.

15
00:00:55.633 --> 00:00:58.733
The gap is a little more than a quarter of the placebo decline.

16
00:00:59.673 --> 00:01:01.413
That share is called the relative slowing.

17
00:01:02.517 --> 00:01:04.757
That share compares two amounts of decline.

18
00:01:05.717 --> 00:01:06.957
It is not a gain in memory.

19
00:01:07.817 --> 00:01:09.497
It is not the share of patients helped.

20
00:01:10.297 --> 00:01:11.857
It is not anyone's prognosis.

21
00:01:14.917 --> 00:01:16.937
Slower worsening is still worsening.

22
00:01:17.937 --> 00:01:21.117
In this trial, a drug that clears amyloid slowed the disease.

23
00:01:22.037 --> 00:01:23.077
It did not stop it.

24
00:01:26.917 --> 00:01:30.597
A second trial tested a different antibody, solanezumab.

25
00:01:31.397 --> 00:01:35.717
It enrolled older adults whose thinking was still normal, but whose brains held

26
00:01:35.717 --> 00:01:36.737
extra amyloid.

27
00:01:38.117 --> 00:01:40.957
On PET scans, amyloid rose in both groups.

28
00:01:41.797 --> 00:01:43.297
It rose more slowly with the drug.

29
00:01:44.267 --> 00:01:47.967
Predict. Did their thinking decline more slowly too?

30
00:01:53.967 --> 00:01:55.287
Both groups declined.

31
00:01:55.287 --> 00:02:00.747
The trial did not demonstrate slower decline with the drug, despite the smaller rise

32
00:02:00.747 --> 00:02:01.747
in amyloid.

33
00:02:02.350 --> 00:02:04.050
ALS shows the same thing.

34
00:02:04.870 --> 00:02:10.390
In people with S .O .D .1, a L .S., a drug called tofersen lowered SOD1.

35
00:02:11.190 --> 00:02:13.710
It also lowered a blood marker of axon damage.

36
00:02:14.733 --> 00:02:18.173
But over twenty-eight weeks, the clinical measures did not improve.

37
00:02:19.233 --> 00:02:20.753
That is the treatment question.

38
00:02:21.653 --> 00:02:23.033
A marker can guide you.

39
00:02:23.473 --> 00:02:27.273
But a clinical outcome is what tells you whether changing the target helps.

40
00:02:28.283 --> 00:02:30.023
Benefits also have costs.

41
00:02:31.043 --> 00:02:34.723
Another antibody, donanemab, was tested in a large trial.

42
00:02:35.663 --> 00:02:40.043
Brain swelling or fluid on MRI was far more common with it than on placebo.

43
00:02:46.167 --> 00:02:48.387
Read a trial as two amounts of decline.

44
00:02:48.387 --> 00:02:52.487
And keep a change in a marker apart from a change in the person.
